# 3. CRITICAL ARCHITECTURAL DECISIONS — MUST BE PRESERVED

These decisions are mandatory. Do not override them during implementation.

## 3.1 Dataset and model selection remain open

No final training dataset or ML model configuration has been approved.

Therefore:

- Do not select a final training dataset on your own.
- Do not download, ingest or train on an unapproved dataset.
- Do not assume that every dataset contains raw email files or complete headers.
- Do not hardcode a specific transformer, classifier or model as the final research model.
- Do not invent training metrics or performance results.
- Do not label illustrative predictions as experimentally validated results.

Build an interchangeable model architecture.

Create a common model interface that can later support:

- TF-IDF with logistic regression.
- TF-IDF with linear SVM.
- Gradient-boosted models using structured features.
- Transformer-based email classifiers.
- Other approved models in future experiments.

The system must support loading, replacing and versioning models without rewriting the application.

Every model prediction must identify:

- Model name.
- Model version.
- Preprocessing version.
- Prediction timestamp.
- Output class or score.
- Whether the score is calibrated.
- Relevant explanation or feature evidence, where available.

If no trained model is configured, the application must clearly display that ML classification is unavailable. It must not fabricate a prediction.

Keep research training and evaluation separate from the operational inference interface.

## 3.2 Persistent storage and deployment remain open

Build the application so it can eventually support production deployment, authentication and persistent storage.

However, the initial implementation must NOT permanently store uploaded emails.

Default privacy behaviour:

- Process uploaded `.eml` files and pasted emails in memory or temporary request-scoped storage.
- Do not permanently save raw email contents.
- Do not permanently save attachments.
- Do not silently retain parsed email bodies or headers.
- Do not send raw email contents to Gemini or third-party enrichment services by default.
- Do not use uploaded emails for model training.
- Clear temporary data when the analysis session ends or expires.
- Do not log email bodies, passwords, tokens or sensitive personal information.

Provide an architecture that can later accommodate:

- Authentication and authorization.
- Persistent investigation records.
- A database.
- Secure evidence retention.
- User roles.
- Audit history.
- Cloud deployment.
- Configurable retention policies.

For now, implement a storage abstraction and an in-memory/session-scoped implementation.

Persistent storage must remain disabled by default.

Do not require a database merely to run the initial application.

Do not invent production credentials or deploy the application without explicit authorization.

